This not only confirms that at least some dinosaurs had feathers—a theory already widely accepted in scientific circles—but also provides information that fossils generally cannot offer, such as color and three-dimensional arrangement.
Scientists say that the ornamental feathers of this animal are dark brown like mahogany on the upper side and yellowish-orange due to carotenoids on the lower side.
This finding also enhances our understanding of the evolution of feathers in birds.
Since this dinosaur lacks well-developed feathers with a shaft—referred to as a quill—but possesses other feather characteristics such as filaments and down-like hairs, researchers therefore argue that the evolutionary development of the shaft likely occurred later than the other feather traits.
Feather structure indicates they were not used for flight, but rather for temperature regulation or display. Researchers believe that even if this specimen belonged to a young dinosaur, it still represents a feather from an adult animal, not the down of a hatchling.
