All the entrances of the cave, known as the "Seven Sisters," have a diameter of at least 100 to 250 meters (328 to 820 feet).
Infrared images show that temperature changes between day and night indicate that these are likely caves.
Their temperature is cooler than the surrounding surface temperature during the day and warmer at night.
According to Glen Koching from the United States Geological Survey (USGS), the team of astronomers at the University of Arizona in Flagstaff, Arizona: "Their temperature behavior is not indicative of the stability of large caves on Earth, where temperatures are typically relatively constant, but rather resembles that of deep pits in the ground."
In France, voting has traditionally been conducted using basic technology: voters would go to individual booths and place a pre-printed paper ballot, indicating their chosen candidate, into an envelope.
After the officials verify the voter's identity, the voter casts the ballot into the ballot box and signs their name in the voting list.
French election law imposes relatively strict regulations on these practices.
Since 1988, ballot boxes must be transparent so that voters and observers can ensure that no envelope is present in them at the beginning of voting and that no envelope other than those of authorized voters is added.
Candidates may appoint representatives to observe any part of the process. At night, votes are counted by volunteers following specific procedures under strict supervision.
