Lions are the most social of all cat species and live in large groups known as prides.
Prairie dog groups consist of one to three related adult males along with about thirty females and their offspring.
Women usually have close relationships with one another and have a large family of sisters and daughters.
Lion groups behave very much like wolves or dogs—animals that remarkably resemble lions (but not other large cats)—and are extremely deadly to their prey.
