When a small group of living organisms (a small population) becomes separated from the main group it belongs to (for example, when they move to the other side of a mountain range or river, or when they go to a new island from which they cannot easily return), they often find themselves in a different environment than their previous one.
This new environment has different resources and competitors, so the new population, in order to become a strong competitor, will need abilities or adaptations different from what they previously required.
The original population has not changed in any way, and they still require the same adaptations to the environment as before.
Over time, as the new population gradually adapts to the new environment, they become increasingly unlike other people.
Eventually, after thousands or even millions of years, the two populations will become so different that they can no longer be considered part of the same species.
We call this process speciation, which refers only to the formation of new species. Speciation is an inevitable outcome and a crucial part of evolution.
