During the Revolutionary War, the first thirteen states under the Confederation principles established a weak central government—Congress being its only component.
The Congress lacked any power to impose taxes, as there was no national enforcement or judicial body, and it had to rely on state authorities, who often did not cooperate, to carry out all its actions.
Also, there was no authority to repeal tax and tariff laws between countries.
Before amending these provisions, unanimous agreement from all countries was required, and countries also had to obtain their central governments' approval, even in the absence of a representative.
