Taiwan's history dates back to the 15th century, when European navigators passing by named the island "Ilha Formosa," meaning "Beautiful Island."
In 1624, the Dutch East India Company established a base in southern-western Taiwan, marking the beginning of a transformation in local grain production and employing Chinese laborers to work on their sugar and rice farms.
In 1683, the Qing Dynasty (1644–1912) forcibly took control of western and northern Taiwan, and in 1885 declared Taiwan as one of the provinces of the Qing Empire.
In 1895, the Qing government, after its defeat in the First Sino-Japanese War (1894–1895), signed the Treaty of Shimonoseki, under which control over Taiwan was ceded to Japan, and Japan ruled the island as a colony until 1945.
