Muhammad was deeply interested in matters beyond this worldly life. He had the habit of going to a cave called "Hira" on Mount "Nur" for contemplation.
The cave itself, having remained intact through the ages, clearly reflects the deep spiritual devotion toward Prophet Muhammad.
This cave is located atop one of the northern mountains of Mecca and is completely isolated from the rest of the world.
In fact, even if someone knows that such a thing exists, finding it is not a simple task. Once you enter the cave, you will be in complete isolation.
Nothing but the clear, beautiful sky above and numerous mountains surrounding can be seen. From inside this cave, most of this world cannot be seen or heard.
The Great Pyramid of Giza is the only one of the Seven Wonders that still stands today.
The Great Pyramid, built by the Egyptians in the third century BCE, is one of several large pyramidal structures constructed in honor of a deceased pharaoh.
Giza Plateau, also known as the "Giza Necropolis," in the Valley of the Kings in Egypt, includes several pyramids (among which the Great Pyramid is the largest), several small tombs, some temples, and the large statue of the Sphinx located in that valley.
The Great Pyramid was built in honor of the pharaoh Khufu, and many smaller pyramids, tombs, and temples were constructed in honor of Khufu's wives and other members of his family.
