The High Middle Ages emerged after the Early Middle Ages, followed by the Late Middle Ages, which ended around 1500 according to convention.
Technological determinism is a term that actually encompasses a wide range of beliefs, from the idea of technological pressure or technological necessity to a more precise meaning suggesting that an inherent logic tied to scientific laws and their emergence in technology governs human destiny.
Most interpretations of technological determinism share two general ideas: that technological development follows a path beyond the influence of culture or politics, and that technology itself exerts greater "impacts" on societies in themselves than on conditional societies.
For instance, someone might say that the automobile necessarily led to road development.
But a nationwide road network is economically unfeasible for a small number of vehicles; therefore, to reduce the cost of car ownership, new production methods have been developed.
Shared car ownership also leads to a higher incidence of road accidents, which in turn results in the development of new methods in healthcare for repairing damaged bodies.
Romanticism had a strong element of cultural determinism, originating from writers such as "Goethe," "Fichte," and "Schlegel."
In romantic literature, geography shapes individuals, and over time, the customs, traditions, and culture associated with that geography emerge, aligning with society's position in a way that is more meaningful than rigid, arbitrary laws imposed by tyrants.
Just as Paris is regarded as the capital of modern global fashion, Constantinople was considered the capital of European fashion during the feudal era.
Its fame began around the year 400 AD due to its status as a center of aristocracy and continued until around the year 1100 AD.
During the 12th century, its position weakened because the Crusaders returned their goods—such as silk and spices, which were worth more than the goods available in the Eastern Roman market.
At the same time, the transfer of the title of capital city from Constantinople to Paris took place.
Gothic style reached its peak between the 10th–11th centuries and the 14th century.
Initially, clothing was mostly influenced by Byzantine culture in the East.
Nevertheless, due to slow communication channels, fashion trends in the West may be 25 to 30 years behind.
In the late Middle Ages, Western Europe began to develop fashion. One of the most significant changes during that time resulted from the Crusades, as people started using buttons to fasten their clothing.
Subsistence farming is farming carried out solely to produce enough food to meet the needs of the farmer and his family.
Subsistence agriculture is a simple system, often organic, that uses locally stored seeds from specific ecosystems along with crop rotation or other relatively simple methods to maximize yield.
Historically, most farmers earned their livelihood through agriculture, and this still exists in many developing countries today.
Subcultures bring together like-minded individuals who feel neglected by societal standards and help them develop a stronger sense of identity.
Subcultures can differ based on the age, ethnicity, social class, place of residence, and/or gender of their members.
Characteristics that distinguish a subculture may be linguistic, aesthetic, religious, political, sexual, geographical, or a combination of these characteristics.
Members of a subculture often express their membership through a distinctive and symbolic use of style, including fashion, mannerisms, and deliberate speech patterns.
One of the most common methods used to illustrate the importance of socialization is referring to cases of children who, during their development, failed to learn socialization due to neglect, abuse, or intentional harm by adults in society.
These children are referred to as "wild children." Some of these wild children are confined by people (usually their own parents); in some cases, the abandonment of the child results from parents' denial of the child's severe mental and physical disabilities.
Wild children may have experienced severe abuse or emotional trauma before running away or being abandoned.
It is claimed that the rest were raised by large animals; it has been said that some lived alone in the wild.
If a child is raised completely by animals other than humans, the wild child will exhibit behaviors (within physical limits) almost identical to those of animals, such as fear and indifference toward humans.
Although project-based learning should make learning easier and more engaging, scaffolding takes it a step further.
Scaffolding is not a learning method in itself, but rather support provided to individuals who are undergoing a new learning experience, such as using a new computer program or starting a new project.
Scaffolds may be virtual or physical; in other words, a teacher is a type of scaffold, but even a small paper clip in Microsoft Office can also be a scaffold.
Virtual scaffolds are embedded within the embedded software, with the aim of prompting, questioning, and explaining methods that might otherwise be challenging for students to handle independently.
Children are placed in foster homes for various reasons. The range of these reasons extends from neglect to abuse, and even exploitation.
No child should be forced to grow up in an environment lacking nourishment, care, and upbringing, yet this is happening.
We consider the child protection system to be a safe environment for these children.
Our nurturing and care system strives to provide safe homes, compassionate caregivers, sustainable education, and reliable healthcare.
For foster parenting, it is essential that all unmet needs of the child from their previous home be fulfilled.
The internet combines elements of interpersonal and collective communication.
The distinctive features of the internet have led to additional dimensions in terms of user approach and satisfaction.
For example, "learning" and "social inclusion" are referred to as important motivations for using the internet (James and Sayerin, 1995).
"Personal involvement" and "ongoing relationships" were also identified by Iyengar and McCord (1998) as new motivational aspects when they investigated audience responses to websites.
The use of video recording has led to significant discoveries in interpreting micro-expressions and facial movements that last only a few milliseconds.
In particular, it is claimed that by correctly interpreting small expressions, one can determine whether a person is lying.
Oliver Sacks, in his article "The President's Speech," demonstrated how individuals who, due to brain injury, are unable to comprehend speech, are still capable of accurately assessing sincerity.
He even suggests that such abilities could be shared in interpreting human behavior among animals like pet dogs.
Twentieth-century research has shown that there are two sources of genetic variation: hidden and expressed.
Mutation increases genetic diversity and selects from the reservoir of expressed diversity.
The separation and recombination of differences between the two pools moves forward and backward with each generation.
Outside the savanna, meeting amino acid requirements from available plant sources has been a difficult task for a primate with a digestive system similar to humans.
Moreover, failing to do so has serious consequences: stunted growth, malnutrition, and ultimately death.
The most accessible plant-based sources of protein are found in leaves and legumes, but digesting these by mammals like us is difficult unless they are cooked.
In contrast, animal-based foods (such as insects, worms, and eggs) are not only easily digestible but also provide high-quality proteins containing all essential amino acids.
Everything has been taken into account, and if our ancestors nearly solved their "protein problem" in the same way that chimpanzees do today in the savanna, we should not be surprised.
Insomnia is the process of becoming consciously awake during normal sleep and then being unable to fall back asleep again after a period of time (10-60 minutes).
This task can be easily accomplished using a relatively gentle alarm clock that wakes you up without fully jolting you from sleep, helping you stay alert.
If you realize that you're turning off the alarm clock while still asleep, you can place it on the other side of the room so that you're forced to get out of bed to turn it off.
Other circadian rhythm-based options include drinking a large amount of fluids (especially water or tea, a known diuretic) before bedtime, which forces the individual to wake up from sleep to urinate.
A person's level of inner calm is inversely related to their level of tension in body and mind.
The lower the tension, the more positive the gift of life becomes. Each person has the ability to find peace and complete satisfaction.
Anyone can achieve enlightenment. The only thing that stands in the way of this goal is our own tension and negativity.
Tibetan Buddhist tradition is based on the teachings of Buddha, but it has been expanded through the Mahayana path of love and numerous techniques drawn from Indian yoga.
Tibetan Buddhism is actually very simple, and includes kundalini yoga, mindfulness meditation, and the path of universal love.
Through Kundalini yoga, Kundalini energy (the energy of enlightenment) is awakened via yoga postures, breathing exercises, mantras, and visualization.
The Tibetan meditation center is the yoga of the gods. Through visualizing various deities, energy channels are purified, chakras are activated, and enlightened awareness is generated.
Germany was a common enemy in World War II, which led to an alliance between the Soviet Union and the United States. After the war ended, differences in system, process, and culture led to the separation of these countries.
Given the last two years of the war, the former allies had become enemies, and the Cold War began.
This was intended to ensure its durability for the next 40 years and to actively engage in real combat alongside allied armies across battlefronts from Africa to Asia, in Afghanistan, Cuba, and many other regions.
By September 17, 1939, Poland's defense had collapsed, and the only remaining hope was retreating and reorganizing along the Romanian corridor.
But these plans nearly collapsed within hours when over 800,000 soldiers from the Red Army of the Soviet Union entered the region, launched an attack on eastern Poland, and established Belarusian and Ukrainian fronts, thereby violating the Treaty of Riga, the Soviet-Polish Non-Aggression Pact, and other bilateral and multilateral international agreements.
Using ships for transporting goods is by far the most efficient way to move large numbers of people and cargo across oceans.
Traditionally, the role of naval forces was to ensure that your country could transport its people and goods, while simultaneously interfering with the enemy's ability to move their people and goods.
One of the examples worth mentioning is the Battle of the North Atlantic during World War II. The Americans sought to transport people and supplies across the Atlantic Ocean to assist Britain.
Meanwhile, the German Navy mainly tried to stop this passage using U-boats.
If Germany had defeated the Allies, it might have been able to conquer Britain since it already held the rest of Europe.
It seems that goats were first domesticated around 10,000 years ago in the Zagros Mountains of Iran.
Ancient cultures and tribes began keeping them for easy access to milk, wool, meat, and skin.
Domestic goats were generally kept in herds on hillsides or other grazing areas and were usually tended by goat herders, who were often children and teenagers, similar to the traditional shepherds. These herding practices are still in use today.
The wagon track was built in 16th-century England.
Although the wagon paths consisted only of parallel wooden planks, they allowed horses to pull them at higher speeds and haul heavier loads compared to dragging cargo over the rougher roads of that time.
Rail fasteners were introduced almost from the beginning to keep the rails in place. Nevertheless, gradually they realized that if a metal clip were placed on the rail fastener, the rails would perform more efficiently.
Although this practice became common, iron caused greater wear on the wooden wheels of the wagons.
Eventually, wooden wheels were replaced by iron ones. In 1767, the first fully iron rails were introduced.
The first form of transportation was walking; humans began walking upright about two million years ago with the emergence of upright humans (Homo Erectus).
Their ancestors were Southerncops who, by custom, normally did not walk on foot.
Bipedalism is evident in southern African fossils from 4.2 to 3.9 million years ago, although the Chad hominins may have been walking on two legs as far back as seven million years ago.
We can start a more friendly life with the environment, join the environmental movement, and even become active in protecting the environment to somewhat reduce future problems.
This is exactly like many other supportive treatments. Nevertheless, if we are not seeking only a temporary solution, we must identify the root causes of the problems and render them inactive.
It is clear that the world has changed due to human scientific and technological advancements, and problems have intensified because of the extremely large population and humans' strange and unusual lifestyles.
After its passage by Congress on July 4th, a handwritten draft bearing the signatures of John Hancock, Speaker of the House, and Charles Thomson, Clerk, was sent to John Dunlap's printing shop, located a few blocks away.
Between 150 and 200 copies were produced during the night, now known as the "Dunlap Declaration."
John Nixon was the one who read the document for the first time on July 8th in front of the public in the courtyard of the "Independence Hall."
On July 6th, a copy was sent to George Washington, who ordered his soldiers in New York to read it on July 9th. A copy reached London on August 10th.
Danelap 25 still exists, and the surviving copies are the oldest versions of this document. The original manuscript has not survived.
Many modern paleontologists believe that a group of dinosaurs survived and are alive today. We call them birds.
Since they are feathered and capable of flight, many people do not recognize them as dinosaurs.
But there are many aspects of birds that still resemble dinosaurs.
They have spiky legs and claws, lay eggs, and walk like a stork on their two hind legs.
Almost all computers currently in use are based on the processing of information that is encoded in the form of binary numbers.
A binary number can only have one of two values, either 0 or 1, and in computer terminology, these digits are referred to as binary digits—or bits.
Internal poisoning is not immediately apparent. Symptoms such as vomiting are so common that it cannot be diagnosed immediately.
The most likely cause of internal poisoning might be the presence of an empty container of medication or a toxic household chemical.
Check the label for specific first aid instructions related to that particular poison.
Entomologists use the term "bug" in a formal sense to refer to this group of insects.
This term is derived from ancient familiarity with bed bugs, which are highly adaptable insects capable of parasitism in humans.
Both the assassin bug and the bed bug are nest-dwelling, meaning they adapt themselves to living in nests or residing within their hosts.
In the United States, there are approximately 400,000 known cases of multiple sclerosis (MS), a significant neurological disease in younger and older adults.
MS is a type of disease that affects the central nervous system, which consists of the brain, spinal cord, and optic nerve.
Research has shown that women are twice as likely to develop MS as men.
A couple might decide that raising a newborn is not beneficial for them or their child.
These couples can arrange a child-rearing plan for their newborn.
In adoption, parents relinquish their parental rights so that another couple can care for the child.
The primary goal of science is to uncover how the world works through the scientific method. This method actually guides most scientific research.
Nevertheless, this is not an isolated case; experimentation is a process that requires asking questions, making observations, and guiding scientific research in order to eliminate one or more possible hypotheses.
Nature philosophers and philosophers focused on ancient texts, and particularly on the Latin versions of the Bible.
Aristotle's views on all scientific matters, including psychology, were accepted.
As Greek knowledge declined, the West severed its ties with its Greek scientific and philosophical roots.
Many regard physiological and behavioral rhythms as largely arising from endogenous cycles and associate their generation with biological clocks.
Circadian rhythms, which are not merely responses to external periodic cues, have been documented in most living organisms, including bacteria, fungi, plants, and animals.
Biological clocks are self-sustaining oscillators that continue a periodic cycle of a free-running rhythm in the absence of external cues.
The Hershey-Chase experiment was one of the most compelling pieces of evidence for the genetic nature of DNA.
"Hershey" and "Chase" used bacteriophages or viruses to insert their DNA into bacteria.
They conducted two experiments in which they labeled the phage DNA with radioactive phosphorus or the phage protein with radioactive sulfur.
A mutation can have highly diverse effects, depending on the type of mutation, the importance of the affected genetic segment, and whether the involved cells are germ line cells or not.
Only mutations in microbial-like cells can be passed on to children, while mutations in other locations may lead to cell death or cancer.
Nature tourism focuses on attracting individuals interested in visiting natural areas, with the aim of enjoying landscapes such as plants and wildlife.
Examples of activities in this area include hunting, fishing, photography, birdwatching, visiting parks, and studying information about the ecosystem.
One example of such an expedition is photography and learning about orchids in Borneo.
Every morning, people from small towns in the county leave their homes by car to go to work, only to be transported by others whose workplace destination is the very same place they came from.
In this dynamic transportation shuttle, everyone is connected to a personal car-based transportation system and supported by it.
Now science shows that this carbon-based economy has pushed the biosphere out of one of its stable states—one that has sustained and supported human evolution over the past two million years.
Everyone in the community participates and uses the transportation systems. Almost everyone complains about the transportation system.
In developed countries, you rarely hear about complaints of similar levels regarding water quality or bridge collapses.
Why have transportation systems caused such complaints, why do we witness their failure every day? Is it due to the inefficiency of vehicle engineers, or is there a more fundamental reason?
Traffic flow is essentially the study of the movement of individual drivers and vehicles between two points and their interaction with each other.
Unfortunately, studying traffic flow is difficult, as driver behavior cannot be predicted with 100% certainty.
Fortunately, drivers tend to behave within a suitable and reasonable range; therefore, traffic flows tend to have a logical structure, which can be approximately determined mathematically.
To better represent traffic flow, relationships among the three defined characteristics have been established: 1) flow, 2) density, and 3) acceleration.
These relationships assist in the planning, design, and operation of road facilities.
Insects were the first group of animals to take to the air. Their ability to fly helped them escape predators more easily and enabled them to find food and mates more effectively.
Most insects have the ability to fold their wings back along the length of their bodies.
This ability enables them to hide from predators across a wider range of smaller spaces.
Today, the only insects that cannot fold their wings behind their backs are midges and one-day-old mosquitoes.
Thousands of years ago, a man named Aristarchus said that the solar system revolves around the Sun.
Some thought he was right, but many people held a contrary belief—that the solar system, including the Sun (and even other stars), revolved around the Earth.
This seems logical, because on Earth you don't feel yourself moving— isn't that right?
The Amazon River is the second longest river in the world and the largest river by volume. The amount of water it carries is eight times greater than that of the world's second-largest river.
The Amazon is also the widest river on Earth, with its width reaching up to six miles in certain places.
Twenty percent of the total water flowing from Earth's rivers into the oceans comes from the Amazon River.
The main channel of the Amazon River is 6,387 kilometers (3,980 miles) long and collects water from thousands of small rivers.
Although the construction of stone pyramids continued until the end of the Old Kingdom, none ever surpassed the Great Pyramids of Giza in size and technical superiority.
The new pharaoh of ancient Egypt was astonished to see the historical structures of their ancestors, which were over a thousand years old.
The population of Vatican City is about 800 people. It is the smallest independent country in the world and also has the smallest population among countries.
The Vatican City uses the Italian language in the fields of legislation and administrative communications.
Italian is also a commonly used daily language by most people working in this state, while Latin is frequently used in religious ceremonies.
All citizens of Vatican City are Roman Catholics.
Since ancient times, people have known basic chemical elements such as gold, silver, and copper, as all these elements can be found in their pure form in nature and can be extracted using relatively simple primitive tools.
Aristotle, the philosopher, established a hypothesis based on the idea that everything is composed of a combination of one or more of the four elements. These elements were earth, water, air, and fire.
More similar to the four states of matter (in the same order): solid, liquid, gas, and plasma, although he also proposed the theory that they transform into new forms of matter, which make up what we observe.
Alloys are essentially mixtures of two or more metals. Keep in mind that there are many elements in the periodic table.
Elements such as calcium and potassium are considered metals. Of course, metals such as silver and gold also exist.
Some alloys also contain certain amounts of non-metallic elements such as carbon.
Everything in this world is made of matter. All materials are composed of tiny particles called atoms.
Atoms are so unbelievably small that trillions of them can fit at the end point of this sentence.
