The modern city of Casablanca was founded by Berber fishermen in the 10th century BC, and the Phoenicians, Romans, and Merinids used it as a strategic port, calling it Anfa.
The Portuguese destroyed it and then rebuilt it under the name of Casablanca, and only after the earthquake of 1755 did they abandon it.
The Sultan of Morocco reconstructed the city of Dar al-Beyda, and the Spanish merchants who established their trading posts there named it Casablanca.
Casablanca is one of the cities in Morocco where shopping destinations have the least appeal.
Around the old Medina, you can find places selling traditional Moroccan goods, such as tagines, pottery, leather items, hookahs, and a full range of patterns, but all these items are meant solely for tourists.
Goma is a tourist city in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, located in the east and near Rwanda.
In 2002, Goma was destroyed by the lava flows from Mount Nyiragongo, which buried most of the city's streets, particularly those in the city center.
Although security in Goma has been established responsibly, any visit outside Goma should be carefully considered to account for the ongoing conflict situation in North Kivu province.
This city is also a base for climbing Mount Nyiragongo, along whose slopes the most accessible gorilla habitats in Africa can be found.
You can use motorcycle taxis to get around the city of Goma. The usual local price for a short ride is about 500 Congolese francs.
The city of "Timbuktu," which had almost no access, has led to its name being used as a metaphor for strange and remote lands.
Today, Timbuktu is a poor city, although its historical significance gives it tourist appeal and it also has an airport.
In 1990, it was added to the list of World Heritage Sites in Danger due to the threat of desert encroachment.
That stop was one of the main stops during Louis Gates' special PBS program titled "The Wonders of the African World."
This city is clearly at odds with other cities in this country, as it is predominantly characterized by Arab traits rather than African ones.
Kruger National Park (KNP) is located in northeastern South Africa and stretches from the eastern border with Mozambique and the northern border with Zimbabwe, with its southern boundary being the Crocodile River.
This park covers an area of 19,500 square kilometers and is divided into 14 distinct biogeographical realms, each supporting different wildlife.
One of the main attractions of South Africa, and considered the flagship flower of South Africa's national parks (SANParks).
Like all national parks in South Africa, daily conservation and entry to this park are also fee-based.
Purchasing a game card that provides access to selected parks in South Africa or all of South Africa's national parks can be beneficial.
Hong Kong Island has given its name to the territory of Hong Kong and is a place that many tourists consider the main focus of their visit.
The skyline of Hong Kong, formed by its buildings, resembles a glowing bar chart, clearly visible alongside the waters of Victoria Harbour.
To get the best view of Hong Kong, leave the island and head toward the opposite shore of Kowloon.
The majority of Hong Kong Island's urban development extends along the northern coastline, densely occupying reclaimed and developed land.
This is a place where the British colonizers seized the land for themselves; therefore, if you are looking for evidence of the colonial past of this land, this is a good place to start.
Sundarbans is the world's largest continuous mangrove forest, stretching 80 kilometers (50 miles) from the coasts of Bangladesh and India inland.
"Sunderbans" has been declared a UNESCO World Heritage site. The forested area located within the Indian territory is known as Sunderbans National Park.
These forests are not just the Karna swamps—they also contain some of the last remaining patches of dense forests that once covered the Sindh and Ganges plains.
The Sundarbans area covers approximately 3,850 square kilometers, with about one-third of it consisting of water and swamps.
Sundarbans has been a wildlife sanctuary since 1966, and it is estimated that currently around 400 royal Bengal tigers and approximately 30,000 deer can be found in this region.
Buses depart throughout the day from the inter-district station (opposite the river), but most of them, especially those heading east and to Jakaar/Bumteng, operate between 6:30 and 7:30 a.m.
Since intercity buses are often full, it is recommended to book tickets a few days in advance.
