Please visit this place with all the dignity, pride, and respect it deserves. Do not make jokes about the Holocaust or the Nazis.
Don't make this place look dull by adding wall paintings or carvings on the structures.
The official languages of Barcelona are Catalan and Spanish. About half of the people prefer to speak Catalan, nearly all of them understand it, and almost everyone knows Spanish.
Nevertheless, most signs are in Catalan only, as it is recognized in law as the primary official language.
Despite this, Spanish is also widely used in public transportation and other places.
Routine announcements in the metro are made only in Catalan, but unexpected disruptions are automatically broadcast in multiple languages, including Spanish, English, French, Arabic, and Japanese.
Parisians are known for their arrogance, rudeness, and pride.
Although this perception gives an inaccurate image of most people, the best way to become friendly in Paris is to be well-mannered and behave like someone who is "bien élevé" (well-bred). In this way, navigating the city becomes notably more pleasant.
If you show some basic courtesy, the seemingly unexpected behavior of Parisians will immediately disappear.
Plitvice Lakes National Park has extensive forest coverage and is predominantly covered with various types of spruce, fir, and beech trees, featuring a mix of Alpine and Mediterranean vegetation.
It has a remarkably wide diversity of plant communities due to its microclimatic variety, diverse soils, and varied altitudes.
This region is also a habitat for a wide variety of animal and bird species.
Rare animals such as the European brown bear, wolf, eagle, owl, blackbird, wild cat, and forest raven can be found here, along with many other common species.
